        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The HRF PC is an IBM 760 XD laptop with a Pentium 166 processor.  It has a 3.1 GB hard drive that is partitioned between two types of software: HRF and EXPRESS.  HRF Common Software provides a platform to launch experiment software and archive data.  When connected to the HRF Rack, it provides downlinking and uplinking capabilities. 
<P>
The HRF PC is used to provide a platform for installing and executing HRF software to perform the various tasks required in experiment procedures.  These tasks include, but are not limited to, command and control of HRF equipment, experiment data collection and storage, data down-link and up-link, crew notes, crew tests, and HRF equipment tests.<BR>

The HRF Portable Computer has the capability to:<BR>
<LI>Provide a 166 MHz Intel Pentium processor with Multimedia Extension (MMX). 
<LI>Provide a removable 3.1GB hard drive, a 3.5" 1.44 MB floppy drive, a removable 8X speed 
        CD-ROM drive.
<LI>PCMCIA hard drive for each crew member's personal log, and be capable of sharing one half of the hard drive with HRF Rack operation software. 
<LI>Provide 64 MB EDO memory RAM. 
<LI>Provide a 12.1" XGA color TFT active matrix display (1024 x 768), 64K color. 
<LI>Provide an external SVGA display interface and a printer port. 
<LI>Receive 28 Vdc power from the Rack, and use it to charge the internal battery. 
<LI>Operate from internal battery power at least one hour under average load before recharge or replacement is required.
<LI>Provide a 5 Vdc auxiliary power source to other HRF hardware items. 
<LI>Provide a built-in full keyboard and integral cursor control for user input. 
<LI>Provide interfaces for cursor control devices (joysticks or other trackballs, and a full-sized keyboard. 
<LI>Provide multiple high speed serial ports (RS-232, RS-422) for data communication with the HRF rack computer and other HRF serial devices. 
<LI>Provide Ethernet network connectivity. 
<LI>Provide multi-channel Analog and Digital Input/Output functions for experiment data acquisition a and control. 
<LI>Provide a restraint device (articulating arm) that can be easily attached to a seat track and can be easily adjusted and then locked into place and provide sufficient stability to accommodate firm typing or cursor control actions for the PC.
<LI>Provide ability to modify HRF PC software from the ground; a user interface for crew, ground support and development personnel; and software to detect computer viruses. 
<LI>Provide software utilities to down-link or up-link data, and communicate with serial devices. 
<LI>Provide software utility to receive space station time and synchronize its internal clock to the space station time. 
<LI>Provide an operating system (MS Windows NT 4.0). 
<LI>Provide ability to accept custom-built or COTS software. 
<LI>Provide Word processing, Graphics, Spreadsheet, Basic statistics, File compacting, on-line calendar function, and data access security software. 
<LI>Provide software to convert and synchronize multiple analog signals to digital and digital to analog to create virtual instruments (e.g., LabViews). 
<LI>Provide interfaces for microphone and headset, built-in stereo speakers, interfaces for video input/output, and enhanced video (IBM MPEG-2) features. 
<LI>Provide two Type II PCMCIA slots. 
<LI>Provide 3Com EtherLink III networking cards, type II PCMCIA Data Acquisition cards (Callunacard, 520 MB) (Flight 7A), type II PCMCIA Serial communication cards (National Instruments RS-232 and RS-422/485), type III PCMCIA hard drive. 
<LI>Provide an expansion PCI Bus interface for system expansion. 
<LI>Provide investigators with the ability to use ground versions of the same computer for software/experiment development activities. 
<LI>Be stowed in the HRF Rack utility drawer or a similar secure location, and be transportable. 
<P>
Additional specifications include:<BR>
Length, height, depth (cm³): 1000<BR>
Mass (kg): 1.6<BR>
